INSERT INTO public.decision_scenarios
  (market_id, concept_key, concept_label, surface, situation, prompt, options, correct_option_index, consequence, mechanism, misconception_tags, difficulty, era_tag)
VALUES
('aerospace','unit_economics_launch','Launch unit economics','lesson','A launch provider can reuse its first stage up to ten times, but each refurbishment costs 30% of a new booster.','As a program lead, what most improves cost per kilogram to orbit?','["Fly each booster more times before retirement","Raise the sticker price per launch","Add a second launch pad","Increase payload insurance coverage"]',0,'Flight count is the lever. Spreading the fixed cost of a booster over ten flights cuts hardware cost per launch far more than pricing or ground infrastructure changes.','Reusable hardware turns a per-launch capital cost into an amortized one. The denominator (flights per vehicle) moves cost per kilogram faster than anything on the revenue side.','{"1":"confuses price with cost","2":"assumes capacity equals efficiency","3":"treats insurance as a cost driver"}','medium','Reusable era'),
('agtech','yield_per_input','Yield per input','lesson','A grower can spend the same budget on precision irrigation sensors or on 15% more fertilizer.','Which choice most reliably raises margin per acre?','["Precision irrigation sensors","More fertilizer","Neither — hold the budget","Expand planted acreage"]',0,'Sensors cut water and input waste immediately, lifting margin without raising variable cost per acre. Extra fertilizer hits diminishing returns fast and can depress yield.','Agricultural inputs follow a diminishing-returns curve. Once near the optimum, information beats volume: knowing where and when to apply beats applying more.','{"1":"assumes more input equals more output","3":"confuses scale with efficiency"}','medium',NULL),
('ai','inference_cost','Inference cost structure','lesson','A product team sees usage triple while gross margin falls.','What is the most likely cause?','["Inference cost scales with usage while pricing is flat per seat","Training cost rose","Fewer users renewed","Support headcount grew"]',0,'Per-seat pricing with usage-based costs inverts the normal software margin curve: every new active user costs real compute.','Traditional software has near-zero marginal cost. AI products carry a variable cost per request, so pricing must track usage or margin erodes as adoption grows.','{"1":"confuses one-off training with ongoing serving cost","2":"contradicts the growth signal"}','medium',NULL),
('biotech','trial_risk','Clinical trial risk','lesson','A company reports strong Phase 1 safety results and the stock doubles.','What does Phase 1 success actually prove?','["The drug appears safe at tested doses, not that it works","The drug works","The drug will be approved","The market is large"]',0,'Phase 1 is a safety and dosing study in a small group. Most Phase 1 successes still fail later on efficacy.','Trial phases answer different questions. Confusing safety evidence for efficacy evidence is the most expensive mistake in biotech investing.','{"1":"reads safety as efficacy","2":"skips remaining phase risk","3":"confuses market size with approval odds"}','easy',NULL),
('cleanenergy','capacity_factor','Capacity factor','lesson','Two projects both quote 100 MW of nameplate capacity: one solar, one geothermal.','Which produces more electricity in a year, and why?','["Geothermal, because its capacity factor is far higher","Solar, because panels are more efficient","They produce the same","Solar, because it has lower cost"]',0,'Geothermal runs near-continuously, while solar output tracks daylight and weather. Same nameplate, very different annual energy.','Nameplate capacity is a maximum, not an output. Capacity factor — actual output divided by theoretical maximum — is what turns megawatts into megawatt-hours.','{"1":"confuses panel efficiency with output hours","2":"ignores capacity factor","3":"confuses cost with production"}','medium',NULL),
('climatetech','abatement_cost','Cost per ton abated','lesson','Two projects each remove carbon: one at $40 per ton at small scale, one at $180 per ton with a clear path to $30 at scale.','Which is the better climate investment today?','["It depends on how credible the scale-up path is","Always the cheaper one today","Always the one with the lower future cost","Neither — both are too expensive"]',0,'Cost per ton today is only half the picture. The decision hinges on whether the learning curve and volume assumptions behind the future price are credible.','Deployment drives cost declines along a learning curve, but only where the technology has real scaling headroom. Judgement about credibility is the actual skill.','{"1":"ignores learning curves","2":"takes projected costs at face value"}','hard',NULL),
('cybersecurity','blast_radius','Blast radius','lesson','A breach review finds one compromised contractor laptop gave access to the production database.','What is the highest-value fix?','["Segment access so one credential cannot reach production","Require longer passwords","Buy more endpoint licenses","Increase security awareness training frequency"]',0,'Segmentation limits blast radius. Even with a compromised device, the attacker reaches far less.','Assume compromise. Controls that reduce what a breached identity can touch outperform controls that only try to prevent the initial compromise.','{"1":"treats credential strength as the constraint","2":"buys tools instead of changing architecture","3":"assumes training prevents all compromise"}','medium',NULL),
('ev','battery_cost','Battery cost per kWh','lesson','A carmaker wants a cheaper mass-market model.','Which change moves the sticker price most?','["Switching to a lower-cost battery chemistry","Adding a larger infotainment screen","Reducing paint colour options","Extending the warranty"]',0,'The pack is the single largest cost in an electric vehicle. Chemistry choice moves price far more than trim or feature decisions.','Battery cost per kilowatt-hour dominates the bill of materials. Where the pack goes, the affordable segment follows.','{"1":"confuses perceived value with cost","2":"targets trivial cost lines","3":"adds cost rather than removing it"}','easy',NULL),
('fintech','take_rate','Take rate','lesson','A payments startup grows volume 5x but revenue only 2x.','What most likely happened?','["The take rate fell as it won larger, lower-margin merchants","Fraud losses rose","Users churned","Interest rates changed"]',0,'Large merchants negotiate lower rates. Volume grew faster than revenue because each dollar processed earns less.','Take rate is revenue divided by payment volume. Enterprise mix shifts compress it, which is why volume growth alone never tells the revenue story.','{"1":"confuses losses with revenue","2":"contradicts the volume growth"}','medium',NULL),
('healthtech','reimbursement','Reimbursement pathway','lesson','A clinically validated remote monitoring product struggles to sell to hospitals.','What is the most likely blocker?','["There is no reimbursement code, so the hospital cannot bill for it","Doctors dislike new technology","The product is not accurate enough","Patients will not use it"]',0,'Without a billing pathway, the hospital absorbs the cost. Clinical evidence does not create a buyer; reimbursement does.','In health systems the payer, not the user, decides adoption. Reimbursement is the market-access gate that sits between validation and revenue.','{"1":"blames culture over economics","2":"assumes an evidence problem","3":"assumes a demand problem"}','medium',NULL),
('logistics','density_economics','Route density','lesson','A delivery network wants lower cost per parcel.','Which change helps most?','["More deliveries per stop and per route","Faster vehicles","Larger warehouses","More delivery windows offered"]',0,'Density is the lever. More parcels per route spread fixed driving time and labour across more deliveries.','Last-mile cost is dominated by time per stop, not distance. Density converts the same route into more revenue-producing deliveries.','{"1":"confuses speed with efficiency","2":"solves a storage problem","3":"adds constraint and cost"}','medium',NULL),
('neuroscience','correlation_causation','Correlation and causation','lesson','A study finds people with higher screen time show lower attention scores.','What can you conclude?','["The two are associated; the direction of cause is unknown","Screens damage attention","Low attention causes screen use","Nothing can be learned from the study"]',0,'Observational association does not establish direction. Lower attention could drive more screen use, or a third factor could drive both.','Causal claims require design — randomization, longitudinal tracking or a natural experiment. Association is a starting hypothesis, not a finding.','{"1":"assumes causation","2":"assumes reverse causation confidently","3":"dismisses valid evidence"}','easy',NULL),
('robotics','payback_period','Automation payback','lesson','A $400,000 robotic cell replaces $150,000 of annual labour cost and needs $30,000 a year in maintenance.','Roughly how long is the payback period?','["About 3 years","About 1 year","About 8 years","It never pays back"]',0,'Net annual saving is $120,000, so a $400,000 system pays back in about 3.3 years — inside the typical industrial threshold.','Automation decisions come down to net annual saving against capital cost. Maintenance and downtime must be subtracted before comparing.','{"1":"ignores maintenance and capital cost","2":"underestimates the saving","3":"ignores the saving entirely"}','medium',NULL),
('spacetech','constellation_economics','Constellation economics','lesson','A satellite operator plans a 300-satellite constellation with 5-year design lives.','What must the business model cover that a single-satellite operator does not?','["Continuous replacement capital every year","Higher launch insurance only","Ground station rent only","Nothing different"]',0,'A large constellation is a treadmill: roughly a fifth of the fleet must be replaced every year just to hold service levels.','Short design lives turn capital expenditure into a recurring cost. Steady-state replacement spend, not the initial build, defines the economics.','{"1":"treats it as an insurance question","2":"treats it as an operating-cost question","3":"misses the replacement treadmill"}','hard',NULL),
('web3','liquidity_depth','Liquidity depth','lesson','A token shows high daily trading volume but a large sell order moves the price 20%.','What does this tell you?','["Volume is high but depth is thin","The token is undervalued","The market is efficient","Volume data is wrong"]',0,'Volume measures turnover; depth measures how much size the order book absorbs without moving price. They are not the same.','Thin order books produce high slippage regardless of headline volume, especially when volume is concentrated in a few wallets or automated trading.','{"1":"reads price impact as mispricing","2":"treats slippage as efficiency"}','medium',NULL);